From: StealthOS TorClient <opensource@olib.ai>
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] dispatch: forget pubsub names when Tor shuts down
Upstream-Status: candidate (not yet submitted)
Applies-To: tor 0.4.9.14

The four dispatch namemaps (message, subsystem, channel and message-type
names -> 16-bit IDs) in dispatch_naming.c are file-level statics that nothing
ever frees. They are hash tables keyed with siphash24g(), and that key is
discarded by crypto_global_cleanup() and re-randomized by the next
crypto_global_init(). After an in-process tor_run_main() restart every entry
of the previous run is filed under a hash the new run can no longer compute.

HT_FIND() only compares names inside the bucket the new hash selects (the
cached hash is not compared), so a lookup of an old name either misses and
allocates a duplicate ID, or, when the new hash happens to land in the stale
entry's bucket, returns the stale ID. HT_INSERT() growth then re-buckets the
table in the middle of pubsub_install(), so one name can resolve to two IDs
within a single run: a publisher and its subscriber end up on different
message IDs, pubsub_builder_check() fails ("has publishers, but no
subscribers"; not visible because logging is not configured yet), and

  pubsub_install() -> tor_assert(r == 0) -> tor_abort_()

The tables also grow by ~5 messages, 2 subsystems and 2 channels per run.

Reproduced on macOS with Tor built from this tarball (+0001, +0002) and
AddressSanitizer: an in-process start/HALT loop aborted in pubsub_install()
in 9 of 10 seeded 100-cycle runs (first abort on the 7th tor_run_main()),
which matches the iOS simulator crash on the 6th restart. With this patch:
10 x 100 and 1 x 400 cycles clean under ASan, and the ID counts read 0 after
every shutdown.

Clear the maps in tor_free_all(), right after the dispatcher and the publish
bindings are gone, restoring their static initial state so each run builds
its IDs under its own siphash key.
---
--- a/src/lib/dispatch/dispatch_naming.h
+++ b/src/lib/dispatch/dispatch_naming.h
@@ -47,5 +47,6 @@
 size_t get_num_msg_type_ids(void);
 
 void dispatch_naming_init(void);
+void dispatch_naming_free_all(void);
 
 #endif /* !defined(TOR_DISPATCH_NAMING_H) */
--- a/src/lib/dispatch/dispatch_naming.c
+++ b/src/lib/dispatch/dispatch_naming.c
@@ -38,6 +38,34 @@
 {
 }
 
+/** Release <b>map</b> and restore its static initial state. */
+static void
+dispatch_naming_reset(namemap_t *map)
+{
+  const namemap_t empty = NAMEMAP_INIT();
+  namemap_clear(map);
+  *map = empty;
+}
+
+/**
+ * Forget every message, subsystem, channel and message-type name.
+ *
+ * The maps are hash tables keyed with siphash24g(), whose key is discarded
+ * by crypto_global_cleanup() and re-randomized on the next initialization.
+ * Entries that survive an in-process tor_run_main() restart are filed under
+ * hashes the next run cannot compute, so the same name can resolve to two
+ * different IDs within one run and pubsub_install() fails. Call this once
+ * the dispatcher is gone, so that each run starts with empty maps.
+ **/
+void
+dispatch_naming_free_all(void)
+{
+  dispatch_naming_reset(&message_id_map);
+  dispatch_naming_reset(&subsys_id_map);
+  dispatch_naming_reset(&channel_id_map);
+  dispatch_naming_reset(&msg_type_id_map);
+}
+
 #ifndef COCCI
 /* Helper macro: declare functions to map IDs to and from names for a given
  * type in a namemap_t.
